Yes. A lot of identity begins as other people’s predictions about you.

They name you, reward certain versions of you, punish others, assign roles, repeat stories, and eventually some of those external descriptions become your internal narrator:

world says “you are X” → you anticipate being treated as X → you behave around X → others see X → story reinforces itself.

That’s a self-fulfilling feedback loop, not necessarily an objective description.

But there’s another layer: you’re also what happened when you encountered that story. What you accepted, rejected, exaggerated, hid, transformed, or deliberately chose to become.

So maybe:

I’m what the world told me I was—until I learned I could answer back.

And eventually the question changes from “Who am I?” to “Which parts of this identity are actually mine?”